# Package digests are computed over DELIVERED BYTES (spec D3.1), so git must
# hand every consumer the same bytes it handed the machine that digested them.
#
# The corpus bundles were written on Windows in text mode: their manifests
# digest CRLF bytes. Git used to store those files LF-normalised (autocrlf),
# so a clone on Linux/macOS - or any checkout with core.autocrlf=false/input -
# failed every package digest (6,914 objects + 49 pinned facet schemas at
# 1.0.0; semantic digests were unaffected, which is the two-digest design
# working, but the validator rightly reports the package mismatch).
#
# The rule now: bundle files are `-text`. Git never converts them, and the
# blobs ARE the digested bytes (CRLF). Every channel therefore delivers the
# same bytes - checkout under any core.autocrlf setting, `git archive`, and
# raw blob access (`git show`, raw URLs, clients that ignore attributes).
# No published digest was re-spelled. The converters write these bytes
# explicitly (oscal_conv_lib.BUNDLE_NEWLINE), so a re-run is byte-identical
# on every OS.
#
# History (register "Amendments - CP-RISK-1" L-1, and "... - raw blobs"):
# the first fix was `text eol=crlf`, which repaired checkouts and archives
# but left the blobs LF, so raw blob access still mismatched (0/15 on the
# IFA bundle). Storing the digested bytes closed that channel (backlog #42).
converted_examples/*/*-core-bundle/** -text

# The skill's example bundle ships inside SKILL_jascon.zip with LF bytes;
# its content-manifest digests those. Keep them LF on Windows checkouts too.
jascon/examples/*.json text eol=lf

# Evidence blobs are content-addressed (CP-CHECK-1, CHECK-15): the file NAME
# is the SHA-256 of its bytes. Git must never re-spell them, whatever the
# platform or core.autocrlf says.
jascon/examples/evidence/** -text

# A kept pack manifest is content-addressed too (CP-ADVICE-1, ADVICE-8): the
# file NAME is the SHA-256 of the vendor's manifest bytes, and the vendor's
# Attestation binds that digest. The worked pack is a bundle: its manifest
# digests the bytes of its objects. Git never re-spells either.
jascon/examples/packs/** -text
jascon/examples/*.dsse -text
jascon/example-packs/** -text
